/*
 TOP-LEVEL SHAPE: elimination of alternatives feeding an inference to the best explanation.
 Four candidate answers to The Question are put up (no conditions; negative valence; best
 interests; expected utility); each is killed by the same epistemic barrier; the failures are
 turned into three adequacy conditions; and the right to revelatory autonomy is offered as the
 one answer that meets them and explains two further data. Nothing here follows the order of
 the text: the adequacy conditions are printed after the refutations but are the joint that
 holds the whole paper together, and the grounding of the right is printed as a reply to an
 objection.

 THE ARGUMENTS INSIDE IT, and what each one is:

   <Valence Refutation>       — the view demands knowledge the epistemic barrier withholds.
   <Clear Cases>              — refutation by counterexample (the would-be killer, the
                                would-be self-mutilator) of the "no conditions" answer.
   <Unknowability Reductio>   — reductio: assume best-interests or expected-utility, add a
                                minimal condition on permissible action, derive that we may
                                NEVER permissibly intervene; but we may; so both are false.
   <Arbitrariness Objection>  — a dilemma about whose interests count, present or future.
   <Diagnosis>                — the common cause: all three fail BECAUSE the choices are
                                transformative.
   <Self-Making Ground>       — grounding argument: self-authorship is the value, and (an
                                imputed bridge) a value of that kind grounds a right.
   <Meets the Adequacy Conditions> — the three constraints, checked one by one.
   <Outweighing Explains the Clear Cases> — the pro tanto duty applied to the two hard cases.
   <Degrees of Self-Making>   — why a cheeseburger and a degree are not on a par.
   <Two Kinds of Autonomy>    — a distinction drawn to defeat the rational-persuasion objection.
   <Conditional Answer>       — right plus correlative duty give the "only if"; a further
                                suggestion gives the "if".
   <Best Answer>              — the inference to the best explanation that the map hangs from.

 FIDELITY POLICY used here, because the line has to be drawn somewhere and a reader should
 know where: `quotation` is used when the claim's text differs from a contiguous span of the
 source only in capitalisation, emphasis markers, or a dropped leading connective that carries
 no hedge and no attribution. Everything else is `paraphrase`, INCLUDING claims made entirely
 of the author's own words where what the claim drops is a hedge ("Perhaps, then...", "it
 seems plausible that...") or the framing that marks a view as canvassed rather than held.
 Those cases carry a `note:` saying what was dropped. The checker's own tolerance is wider
 than this, so it will report roughly thirty claims as under-marked; that is deliberate.

 TAGS (written here without their hashes, so as not to inflate the tag census): "core" is the
 spine — the twelve arguments, the four rival answers' verdicts, the three adequacy
 conditions, the right, the duty and the answer — so that selectedTags ["core"] is an overview
 rather than the whole file. "detail" is the supporting material each argument runs on: cases,
 examples, sub-premises. "dispute" marks a position the author does not hold, "background" the
 definitions, "scope" a limit the author sets himself.
*/

# The question {isGroup: true}

[the-question]: Under what conditions, if any, is it morally permissible to interfere to try to prevent another from making a transformative choice? #core
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 4",
     source: "\"Under what conditions, if any, is it morally permissible to interfere to try to prevent another from making a transformative choice?\""}

[epistemically-transformative]: An experience is epistemically transformative just in case it provides some knowledge or understanding that one can possess only if one undergoes that experience. #background
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 3",
     source: "\"it provides some knowledge or understanding that one can possess only if one undergoes that experience\""}

[personally-transformative]: An experience is personally transformative just when it changes the core preferences, values and desires of whoever undergoes it. #background
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 3",
     source: "\"it changes the core preferences, values and desires of whoever undergoes it\""}

[transformative-experience]: A transformative experience is one that is both epistemically and personally transformative. #background
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 3",
     source: "\"is one that is both epistemically and personally transformative\""}

[interpersonal-questions-unexplored]: Focus upon first-personal transformative decision-making has left unexplored the ethical questions about how we may treat others who face transformative choices. #background
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 4",
     source: "\"They present an urgent ethical challenge that focusing upon first-personal transformative decision-making has left unexplored.\""}

[interference-any-form]: Interference covers coercion, manipulation, rational persuasion and force alike. #scope
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 5",
     source: "\"In what follows, 'interference' refers to any of these.\"",
     note: "The preceding sentence lists the four: 'Charlie might coerce, manipulate, rationally persuade or force Adam not to become a parent.'"}

[scope-voluntary-only]: The paper concerns only voluntary transformative experiences; involuntary and nonvoluntary ones are set aside. #scope
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "n. 1",
     source: "\"I am concerned only with the voluntary\"",
     note: "The author's own restriction, not an objection: 'The others raise different ethical questions I hope to pursue elsewhere.' Recorded as an attack only because that is the edge Argdown has for a limit on a claim's scope."}

[the-question]
    + [interpersonal-questions-unexplored]

[transformative-experience]
    + [epistemically-transformative]
    + [personally-transformative]

# The epistemic barrier {isGroup: true}

[epistemic-barrier]: There is an epistemic barrier between the time before and the time after a transformative choice. #core
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 7",
     source: "\"Owing to the epistemic barrier between the time before and after a transformative choice\""}

[epistemic-barrier]
    + [epistemically-transformative]
    + [transformative-experience]

# Four answers that fail {isGroup: true}

[no-conditions-view]: There are no conditions under which we may permissibly interfere in another's transformative choice. #dispute
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"Should we thus conclude that we may *never* permissibly interfere in a transformative choice\"",
     note: "Raised by the author as the answer the epistemic barrier seems to force, and immediately rejected: 'This is too quick.'"}

[valence-view]: We may permissibly interfere with another's transformative choice just when that experience will be a negative epistemically or personally transformative experience. #dispute
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 5",
     source: "\"Perhaps, then, we may permissibly interfere with another's transformative choice just when that experience will be a negative epistemically or personally transformative experience.\"",
     note: "The author's 'Perhaps, then' marks this as a candidate entertained, not a view held."}

[best-interests-view]: It is permissible to interfere with another's transformative choice just when it is in the best interests of that person for you to do so. #dispute
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"it is permissible to interfere with another's transformative choice just when it is in the *best interests* of that person for you to do so\"",
     note: "Left as paraphrase although the words are the author's: the claim drops the 'A second view, then:' that marks this as a view canvassed rather than held."}

[expected-utility-view]: We may permissibly intervene in another's transformative choice just when the choice will not maximize the chooser's expected utility, and not otherwise. #dispute
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"we may permissibly intervene just when the choice will not maximize expected utility and not otherwise\""}

[exhaustive-rivals]: These four are the only prima facie plausible answers to The Question. #core
    {fidelity: "imputation", warrant: "enthymeme",
     note: "The author says he considers 'three prima facie plausible answers' plus the 'no conditions' answer, and never claims the list is complete. An elimination of alternatives needs the completeness claim, and this is the weakest joint in the paper."}

# Against the no-conditions answer {isGroup: true}

[would-be-killer]: Someone might wish to have the transformative experience of going on a killing spree. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"suppose someone wished to have the transformative experience of going on a killing spree\"",
     note: "The sentence continues 'or to cut off their body from their waist down'; that second disjunct is not dropped but recorded separately as its own case."}

[would-be-self-mutilator]: Someone might wish to have the transformative experience of cutting off their body from the waist down. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"or to cut off their body from their waist down\""}

[clearly-permitted-to-prevent]: Regardless of not knowing what these experiences will be like for them, we clearly are permitted to prevent them from making these choices. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"Regardless of not knowing what this will be like for them, we clearly *are* permitted to prevent them from making *these* choices.\""}

[we-can-sometimes-intervene]: We can sometimes permissibly intervene in another's transformative choice. #core
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 6-7",
     source: "\"Since we can, these views are false\"",
     note: "Compressed from the two counterexample cases on p. 6 and the 'Since we can' of p. 7, which are two pages apart; the claim is the fixed point both passages rely on."}

<Clear Cases>: Refutation by counterexample: two choices we are plainly permitted to prevent, despite knowing nothing about what they would be like for the chooser. #core
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence",
     note: "The author presents the two cases as a rhetorical aside ('This is too quick'); numbering them as a refutation is the reconstructor's assembly."}

(1) [would-be-killer]
(2) [would-be-self-mutilator]
(3) [clearly-permitted-to-prevent]
-----
(4) [we-can-sometimes-intervene]

[no-conditions-view]
    - [we-can-sometimes-intervene]

# Against the valence answer {isGroup: true}

[valence-unknown-until-after]: In paradigmatic cases of transformative experiences we do not know, and have no good reason to believe, whether they will be positive or negative until after they have happened. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 5",
     source: "\"there are paradigmatic cases of transformative experiences where we *do not know* or we have *no good reason to believe* that they will be positive or negative until *after* they have happened\""}

[third-party-no-better]: A third party who has undergone the same type of experience is not thereby in a position to know what it would be like for this person. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"there is no reason to think that what it is like\"",
     note: "Full sentence: 'there is no reason to think that what it is like for Charlie to hold his child is the same as what it would be like for Adam to hold his child' — quoted short because the source italicises four phrases inside it."}

[barnes-difference]: The experiences leading up to a transformative experience, and the differences between its two subjects, make it unlikely that it will be transformative in the same way for both. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"Given the variation in the experiences that lead up to transformative experiences, and the differences in subjects, this point generalizes\"",
     note: "Akhlaghi is quoting Barnes 2015: 175 here."}

[valence-unknowable]: We cannot know the value of such experiences for individuals in many paradigmatic cases. #detail
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 6",
     source: "\"we cannot know the value of such experiences for individuals in many paradigmatic cases\""}

[permissibility-needs-possessable-grounds]: Permissible interference cannot generally depend upon knowledge, or reasons for belief, that one cannot possess before the choice is made. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 5",
     source: "\"makes it implausible to think that permissible interference generally depends upon knowledge, or reasons for belief, that one *cannot possess* before that choice is made\""}

[valence-view-fails]: The valence answer fails. #core
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 5-6"}

[ambivalent-cases]: The valence answer is also troubled by ambivalent transformative experiences, where it is unclear whether the experience was relevantly positive or negative. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "n. 3",
     source: "\"where it is unclear whether the experience was relevantly positive or negative\""}

<Valence Refutation>: The valence answer demands exactly the knowledge the epistemic barrier withholds. #core
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence",
     note: "The author runs the barrier point and the Barnes generalisation as consecutive paragraphs without numbering them; the two-step structure is the reconstructor's."}

(1) [valence-unknown-until-after]
(2) [third-party-no-better]
-----
(3) [valence-unknowable]
(4) [permissibility-needs-possessable-grounds]
-----
(5) [valence-view-fails]

