# Against the best-interests and expected-utility answers {isGroup: true}

[interests-knowable-only-after]: We can only know what the interests of the future self are, and whether one's present interests will be fulfilled, after a transformative choice has been made. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 7",
     source: "\"we can only know *what* the interests of the future selves are and *whether* one's present interests will be fulfilled *after* a transformative choice has been made\""}

[utilities-knowable-only-after]: Since the utilities of a transformative experience partly depend upon what it would be like for the chooser, and she can only know that after undergoing it, we cannot know what its expected utility is. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 7",
     source: "\"since she can only know this *after* going, how could we know *what* the expected utility of her going to university is?\""}

[depend-on-unpossessable-knowledge]: Both answers make the permissibility of interference depend upon knowledge or reasons for belief we cannot possess at the time of choosing. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 7",
     source: "\"by making the permissibility of interference dependent upon knowledge or reasons for belief we cannot possess at the time of choosing\""}

[cannot-know-when-permitted]: Both answers entail that we can never know or have good reason to believe that we can permissibly interfere in a transformative choice. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 7",
     source: "\"both views entail that we can never know or have good reason to believe that we can permissibly interfere in a transformative choice\""}

[minimal-condition]: There is a minimal condition on acting permissibly that one has good reason to believe the conditions that make one's action permissible are met. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 7",
     source: "\"there is a minimal condition on acting permissibly that one has good reason to believe the conditions that make your action permissible are met\"",
     note: "Footnote 5 records what happens if this condition is rejected: the two views then have to say we can permissibly interfere but can never know when."}

[never-permissibly-intervene]: Both answers entail that we can never permissibly intervene in a transformative choice. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 7",
     source: "\"they also entail that we can *never* permissibly intervene in a transformative choice\""}

[both-views-false]: The best-interests and expected-utility answers are false. #core
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 7",
     source: "\"Since we can, these views are false\""}

<Unknowability Reductio>: Reductio: grant either answer, add the minimal condition on permissible action, and it follows that we may never permissibly intervene — which we may. #core
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence",
     note: "The author labels these 'two serious problems' in prose; the reductio shape, and the placing of the minimal condition as the second premise, are the reconstructor's."}

