# The right to revelatory autonomy {isGroup: true}

[right-to-revelatory-autonomy]: The moral right to autonomously decide to discover how one's life will go and who one will become by making a transformative choice. #core
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 8",
     source: "\"The moral right to autonomously decide to discover how one's life will go and who one will become by making a transformative choice.\""}

[revelatory-non-interference]: The moral duty not to interfere in the autonomous self-making of others, through their choosing to undergo transformative experiences to discover who they will become. #core
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "pp. 8-9",
     source: "\"The moral duty not to interfere in the autonomous self-making of others, through their choosing to undergo transformative experiences to discover who they will become.\""}

[not-mere-autonomy]: The right to revelatory autonomy is not just a right to autonomy but a right to make specific autonomous choices, confronted at a given time, to have revealed to us who we will become. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 8",
     source: "\"it is a right to make *specific* autonomous choices that we are confronted with *at a given time* to have revealed to us, through making a transformative choice, who we will become\""}

[future-self-right-is-separate]: Insofar as a future version of someone has such a right, that right concerns only the transformative choices they may face in the future. #detail
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 8",
     source: "\"Insofar as a future version of someone has such a right, that right concerns only the transformative choices *they* may face in the future\""}

[self-authorship]: Autonomously making transformative choices when facing them, deciding for ourselves to learn who we will become, gives us a degree of self-authorship. #detail
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 8",
     source: "\"autonomously making transformative choices when facing them, deciding for ourselves to learn who we will become, gives us a degree of *self-authorship*\""}

[self-authorship-crucial]: Some degree of self-authorship in this sense is crucial for us and others to see ourselves as ourselves. #detail
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 8",
     source: "\"some degree of self-authorship in this sense is crucial for us and others to see ourselves as\"",
     note: "Quoted short: the source italicises the first syllable of 'ourselves' at exactly this point."}

[value-of-self-making]: There is moral value in autonomous self-making. #core
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 8",
     source: "\"The answer, I suggest, is the moral value of *autonomous self-making*.\""}

[value-grounds-right]: A moral value of this kind grounds a moral right to what realizes it. #core
    {fidelity: "imputation", warrant: "enthymeme",
     note: "The author writes that the value of autonomous self-making 'grounds the right to revelatory autonomy' but never states the principle taking him from a value to a right. The step from the value to the right is invalid without it."}

[nothing-valuable-in-mere-choosing]: There is nothing specifically morally valuable about being allowed to make a free choice in general. #dispute
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 8",
     source: "\"Perhaps there is nothing specifically morally valuable about being allowed to make a free choice in general\"",
     note: "Put by the author as an objection, with O'Neill 2003: 3 cited for the doubt that 'mere, sheer independence or choosing is morally important'."}

[not-choice-as-such]: What is morally valuable is not making a choice as such but autonomously making choices to learn what our core preferences and values will become. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 8",
     source: "\"but, rather, that of autonomously making choices to learn what our core preferences and values will become\""}

<Self-Making Ground>: A grounding argument: self-authorship is what the right protects, and a value of that kind is what generates a right. #core
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "enthymeme",
     note: "The author's own summary is 'It is the value of autonomously self-making that grounds the right to revelatory autonomy.' The bridge premise is supplied here."}

