[duty-is-pro-tanto]: The duty of revelatory non-interference is pro tanto: it can be outweighed by competing moral considerations. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 9",
     source: "\"it can be outweighed by competing moral considerations\"",
     note: "Full sentence: 'Our corresponding duty of non-interference then is pro tanto (Ross 1930, 1939); it can be outweighed by competing moral considerations.'"}

[killer-right-outweighed]: The would-be killer's right to revelatory autonomy is plausibly outweighed by the wrongness of killing others solely to discover who one would become by doing so. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 9",
     source: "\"their right to revelatory autonomy is plausibly outweighed by the wrongness of the killing of others done solely to discover who one would become by doing so\""}

[mutilator-right-outweighed]: The moral reasons to protect one's friends from gratuitous harm plausibly outweigh the would-be self-mutilator's right to discover who they would become. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 9",
     source: "\"the strength of the moral reasons to protect one's friends from gratuitous harm plausibly outweighs their right to autonomously discover what it would be like to irreversibly harm themselves\""}

[explains-permitted-cases]: The view explains how and why there are cases in which it is morally permissible to interfere in another's transformative choice, despite the epistemic barrier. #core
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 9",
     source: "\"my answer explains *how and why* there are cases where it is morally permissible to interfere in another's transformative choice, despite the epistemic barrier surrounding transformative experiences\""}

[rivals-cannot-explain]: By making permissibility depend upon knowledge we cannot possess prior to the making of a transformative choice, the other answers cannot explain this. #core
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 9",
     source: "\"By making the permissibility of interference depend upon knowledge we cannot possess prior to the making of a transformative choice, the other views considered cannot explain this\""}

<Outweighing Explains the Clear Cases>: The pro tanto duty applied to the two cases that defeated the no-conditions answer. #core
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence"}

(1) [duty-is-pro-tanto]
(2) [killer-right-outweighed]
(3) [mutilator-right-outweighed]
-----
(4) [explains-permitted-cases]

[varying-difficulty]: Some cases of interference in transformative choices are easier to justify than others. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 9",
     source: "\"it seems plausible that some cases of interference in transformative choices are easier to justify than others\"",
     note: "Left as paraphrase although the words are the author's: the claim drops the author's hedge, 'it seems plausible that'."}

[cheeseburger-vs-university]: What must obtain for interference in someone's choice to eat a cheeseburger for the first time to be permissible is much less demanding than for interference in their choosing to go to university. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 9",
     source: "\"what must obtain for it to be permissible to interfere in someone's choice to eat a cheeseburger for the first time seems much less demanding than interference in their choosing to go to university\""}

[core-affecting-choices-more-valuable]: The transformative choices that are most likely to affect one's core preferences, identity and values are the more morally valuable ones. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "pp. 9-10",
     source: "\"Those that are most likely to affect your *core* preferences, identity and values\""}

[explains-varying-difficulty]: The view explains why interference in some transformative choices is harder to justify than others. #core
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 9-10",
     source: "\"As going to university is more likely to affect these than eating a cheeseburger for the first time, the strength of the moral reasons for interference must be much greater for the former than for the latter\""}

[other-factors]: The view also allows that the nature of the interference engaged in, and whether it was solicited, can play a part in what makes some cases harder to justify than others. #scope
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "n. 8",
     source: "\"the nature of the interference engaged in, and whether the interference was solicited, can play a part in what makes some cases of interference harder to justify than others\""}

<Degrees of Self-Making>: If the right is grounded in self-making, its weight tracks how far a choice reaches into the core of a self. #core
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence"}

(1) [varying-difficulty]
(2) [value-of-self-making]
(3) [core-affecting-choices-more-valuable]
-----
(4) [explains-varying-difficulty]

[varying-difficulty]
    + [cheeseburger-vs-university]

[explains-varying-difficulty]
    + [other-factors]

# The rational-persuasion objection {isGroup: true}

[rational-persuasion-cannot-violate]: Rational persuasion cannot violate an agent's right to revelatory autonomy, since it offers reasons, evidence and arguments and aims to promote rational decision-making. #dispute
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"respects an agent's right to make autonomous decisions of any kind, since it involves offering reasons, evidence and arguments, and aims to promote (or at least not undermine) rational decision-making\"",
     note: "The worry has bite because 'rational persuasion is the form of interference most likely to be pursued in the cases used to motivate this paper'."}

[autonomy-simpliciter]: Respecting autonomy simpliciter is respecting someone's ability to be a competent, capable reasoner. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"respecting someone's ability to be a competent, capable reasoner\""}

[revelatory-autonomy-is-distinct]: Respecting revelatory autonomy is respecting a person's right to make a specific decision, at a given time, to learn who they will become through a self-making, transformative choice. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"their right to make a specific decision, at a given time, to learn who they will become through a self-making, transformative choice\""}

[persuasion-respects-only-the-first]: Rational persuasion respects an agent's ability to be a competent, capable reasoner, but does not entail respecting their autonomous self-authorship. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"Rational persuasion does respect an agent's ability to be a competent, capable reasoner.\"",
     note: "The second half is the next sentence: 'But it does not entail respecting an agent's autonomous self-authorship.' A footnote marker separates them, so they cannot be quoted as one span."}

[tsai-timing]: One can offer a rational argument against a choice at a time or in a way that prevents an agent from exercising epistemic autonomy, for example too early or too forcefully in their deliberative process. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"offering it, for example, too early or too forcefully in an agent's deliberative process\"",
     note: "Akhlaghi credits Tsai 2014 for the point."}

[feigned-privilege]: One can offer apparent reasons, arguments or evidence as if one were in an epistemically privileged position with respect to what the choice would be like for the agent. #detail
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"trying to offer apparent reasons, arguments or evidence as if one is in an epistemically privileged position with respect to what some choice would be like for an agent\""}

[feigned-privilege-disrespects]: That would constitute a distinctive disrespect of an agent's autonomous self-making whilst respecting their capacity for autonomous reflection. #detail
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"That would constitute a distinctive disrespect of an agent's autonomous self-making whilst respecting their capacity for autonomous reflection\""}

[persuasion-can-violate]: Even rational persuasion can disrespect an agent's right to revelatory autonomy. #core
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"Even rational persuasion, then, can disrespect an agent's right to revelatory autonomy\""}

<Two Kinds of Autonomy>: A distinction between respecting a reasoner and respecting a self-maker, drawn so that rational persuasion can fall on the wrong side of it. #core
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence"}

(1) [autonomy-simpliciter]
(2) [revelatory-autonomy-is-distinct]
(3) [persuasion-respects-only-the-first]
-----
(4) [persuasion-can-violate]

[persuasion-respects-only-the-first]
    + [tsai-timing]
    + [feigned-privilege-disrespects]

[feigned-privilege-disrespects]
    + [feigned-privilege]

[necessary-condition-on-all-interference]
    - [rational-persuasion-cannot-violate]

[rational-persuasion-cannot-violate]
    - [persuasion-can-violate]

# The inference the paper rests on {isGroup: true}

[scope-strength-unsettled]: A complete answer requires ascertaining precisely how strong the right to revelatory autonomy is and what competing considerations can outweigh it, which the paper leaves for another time. #scope
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 10",
     source: "\"A complete answer to our question requires ascertaining precisely how strong the right to revelatory autonomy is and what competing considerations can outweigh it\"",
     note: "The author's own admission of incompleteness — 'These are questions for another time' — not an objection from elsewhere. Recorded as an attack because that is the only edge Argdown has for a limit on a claim."}

<Best Answer>: Inference to the best explanation: the rivals are exhausted and all fail; this answer meets the conditions their failure generates and explains two things they cannot. #core
    {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence",
     note: "The paper never labels its top-level move. This assembles the elimination in section 3, the adequacy conditions, and the two explanatory advantages into the single inference they jointly make."}

